course: books
lesson: 54
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VIII, part 3: Toad's Adventures (no money, no pocket, and the engine-driver)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Free but dizzy with success, Toad hurries away from the town where the real washerwoman is so well known, and sees the red and green lights of a railway station: just what he needs, and a train for home leaves in half an hour.
explain: At the booking-office he reaches for his waistcoat pocket — and finds no money, no pocket and no waistcoat: he left them all in his cell, with his pocket-book, keys, watch and everything else.
explain: He tries his grand old manner, «I'm well-known in these parts», but the clerk laughs at the «madam», and an old gentleman pushes him away and calls him «my good woman», which makes Toad angrier than anything.
explain: Crying on the platform, he wonders about hiding under a seat, when a kind engine-driver says «Hullo, mother! What's the trouble?» Toad tells him a tale of lost money and hungry children at home.
explain: The driver offers a ride on his engine if the «washerwoman» will wash a few of his dirty shirts. Toad has never washed a shirt in his life and doesn't mean to start, but he climbs happily into the cab, planning to send money instead: «it'll be the same thing, or better», he thinks.
fact: Toad leaves «as quickly as possible» because «the lady he was forced to represent was so well-known and so popular a character» in that town.
fact: He sees «red and green lights» and hears «the puffing and snorting of engines and the banging of shunted trucks» (shunted = pushed from one track to another): a railway station.
fact: He is glad not to go through the town and «support this humiliating character by repartees» (repartees = quick clever answers).
fact: A train «bound more or less in the direction of his home» is due to start «in half-an-hour».
fact: The booking-office is where you buy tickets; Toad asks for the station nearest to «the village of which Toad Hall was the principal feature».
fact: Struggling with the cotton gown, he reaches «where all waistcoat pockets are eternally situated» and finds «not only no money, but no pocket to hold it, and no waistcoat to hold the pocket!»
fact: He had left his coat and waistcoat in the cell, «and with them his pocket-book, money, keys, watch, matches, pencil-case — all that makes life worth living».
fact: The book jokes that pockets make «the lord of creation» (people) different from animals that «hop or trip about» with no pockets — a funny way of laughing at Toad's love of his things.
fact: In «a blend of the Squire and the College Don» (a squire is a country landowner, a don a university teacher) he says: «I find I've left my purse behind ... I'll send the money on to-morrow? I'm well-known in these parts.»
fact: The clerk laughs: «I should think you were pretty well known in these parts ... if you've tried this game on often»; «you're obstructing the other passengers» (obstructing = blocking).
fact: An old gentleman pushes him away and calls him «his good woman, which angered Toad more than anything»: proud Toad hates being taken for a poor working woman, an old snobbish feeling.
fact: Toad blames «the pettifogging mistrustfulness of paid officials» (pettifogging = fussy about small things) — really it is his own fault.
fact: The engine-driver is «a burly man with an oil-can in one hand and a lump of cotton-waste in the other»; he calls Toad «mother», a friendly old word for an older woman.
fact: Toad lies: he has «Any amount of 'em» (children) at home, «playing with matches — and upsetting lamps, the little innocents!»
fact: The driver's offer: «If you'll wash a few shirts for me when you get home, and send 'em along, I'll give you a ride on my engine. It's against the Company's regulations» (the railway company's rules); driving an engine makes «a power of shirts» dirty and his missus is «fair tired of washing 'em»; «a power of shirts» = lots of shirts; «missus» = wife.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «skint» (UK) = with no money; «sus» = suspicious; «aye» (Scotland) = yes; «loonie» (Canada) = a one-dollar coin; «no cap» = honestly.
exercise: Why does Toad hurry away from that town? | The real washerwoman is too well known there | The castle bells are ringing for an escape | A policeman has started following him | He wants to find the inn in the high street and eat a big supper
exercise: What tells Toad there is a railway station nearby? | Red and green lights and the puffing of engines | A big signpost saying TO THE TRAINS in gold letters | A crowd of people running with heavy suitcases | The smell of coal smoke coming over a hedge
exercise: Why is Toad glad not to go through the town? | He'd have to keep answering jokes like a washerwoman | The town is full of motor-cars that might run him down | The town has a second castle with even worse dungeons | The townspeople throw carrots at anyone in a bonnet
exercise: How long until the train leaves? | Half an hour | Five minutes or so | Two whole hours | Until tomorrow morning
exercise: What is a booking-office? | Where you buy tickets | Where books are kept | Where engines are mended | Where police write reports
exercise: What does Toad find when he reaches for his waistcoat pocket? | No money, no pocket and no waistcoat | A hole, and all his gold fallen through it | The gaoler's keys, which he took by mistake | A note from the girl saying «Good luck»
exercise: Where did Toad leave his coat and waistcoat? | In his cell in the castle | At the Red Lion inn | At Toad Hall, in his wardrobe | With the washerwoman's niece
exercise: Which of these was NOT left behind in the cell? | His motor-car | His pocket-book | His gold watch | His pencil-case
exercise: How does Toad try to get a ticket without money? | Says he's well known and will pay tomorrow | Offers to wash the clerk's shirts in return for a ticket | Pretends to faint, hoping the clerk will feel sorry | Tries to climb through the ticket window at night
exercise: Which «manner» does Toad put on? | A mix of a squire and a college teacher | A sailor's rough shouting and swearing at the clerk | A baby's crying | A soldier's salute
exercise: What does the clerk say? | That Toad must be well known if he tries this game often | That Toad can travel free because he is so famous | That the train is full and nobody else may get on | That washerwomen always travel at half price on Thursday nights in that town
exercise: What angers Toad most that evening? | Being called «my good woman» | Losing his gold watch and his keys | Missing his supper at Toad Hall | Being laughed at by the girl
exercise: What does «pettifogging» mean? | Fussy about small things | Kind and generous | Lost in the fog and the rain | Fond of pets
exercise: Who is really to blame for Toad's trouble at the ticket window? | Toad himself | The clerk, who was rude | The old gentleman | The washerwoman
exercise: What way of travelling free does Toad think of? | Squeezing under a seat, as he'd seen schoolboys do | Riding on the roof of the last carriage | Hiding in the coal-truck under a sack | Climbing into the guard's van and hiding among the parcels and the milk-churns
exercise: What is the engine-driver doing when Toad meets him? | Oiling, wiping and caring for his engine | Eating a pork pie on the platform bench | Shouting at a porter about the late train | Sleeping in his cab with his cap over his eyes
exercise: What does the engine-driver call Toad? | «Mother» | «Good sir» | «Your Honour» | «Little toad»
exercise: What story does Toad tell the driver? | He is a washerwoman who lost her money, with children waiting | He is a rich toad escaping from a castle dungeon | He is a ticket inspector checking the engine | He is the driver's long-lost cousin, home at last from a voyage at sea
exercise: «Any amount of 'em,» sobs Toad. Any amount of what? | Children | Shirts to wash | Sovereigns | Motor-cars
exercise: What does the engine-driver offer? | A ride on his engine, if Toad washes some shirts | Money for a ticket, if Toad cooks his supper | A seat in first class, if Toad sings a song | A bed for the night at his house, if Toad mends all his socks
exercise: Why does the driver need shirts washed? | Driving an engine is dirty work and his wife is tired of washing | He is going to a wedding the next morning | His wife has gone away to visit her sister | He wants to sell clean, ironed shirts at the market on Saturday morning
exercise: Is the ride allowed by the railway company? | No, it's against the Company's rules | Yes, washerwomen ride free | Yes, if they sit on the coal | Only on Sundays, with a special ticket
exercise: What does Toad really plan to do about the shirts? | Never wash them, but send money for washing later | Wash them very carefully himself at Toad Hall | Ask the washerwoman aunt to wash them | Throw them all in the river on the way home, and say they blew away
exercise: Looking back: in chapter II, how did the Rat and the Mole take Toad home after the caravan crash? | By a slow train, after leaving him in the waiting-room with a porter | By motor-car, which Toad drove himself all the way back | By boat, rowing up the river through the night | On the old grey horse, all three of them riding on its back the whole way home
exercise: Looking back: in chapter II, what did Toad say was his motto in life? | «Live for others!» | «Poop-poop for ever!» | «Never tell a lie!» | «Home is best!»
exercise: Looking back: in chapter VI Toad also lied to get his way. What did he pretend then? | That he was dying and needed the doctor and a lawyer | That he was a washerwoman with a dozen children | That he had sold Toad Hall to Badger | That the Rat had stolen his money and sold his best motor-cars to the police
exercise: ★★★ Count the things Toad left in the cell, as the book lists them after the coat and waistcoat: pocket-book, money, keys, watch, matches, pencil-case. How many is that? | Six | Five | Seven | Four
exercise: ★★★ «Not only no money, but no pocket to hold it, and no waistcoat to hold the pocket!» Which chain matches that order? | Money is held by a pocket, which is held by a waistcoat | A waistcoat is held by a pocket, which holds money | Money holds the pocket, which holds the waistcoat | The pocket holds the waistcoat, and the waistcoat holds all the money
test: «Toad was skint cos he spent it all on the aunt, no cap.» (skint = with no money) Put it right. | He had money, but left it in his coat in the cell | Yes, he gave the aunt every single penny he had | He was robbed by a fox on the way to the station | The girl took all his money to pay for the tea and the buttered toast
test: «Clerk sold Toad a ticket cos he's famous.» Put it right. | The clerk laughed and sent him away from the window | The clerk gave him a ticket and a cup of tea | The clerk knew him and called the police | The clerk was asleep, so Toad took a ticket himself
test: Hamish from Dundee: «aye, the auld gentleman was polite tae Toad.» (aye = yes; auld = old; tae = to) Put it right. | He prodded Toad, pushed him off and called him «my good woman» | He bowed and called him «Mr. Toad of Toad Hall» | He kindly paid for Toad's ticket home out of his own purse | He offered Toad his seat in the first-class carriage
test: Which is TRUE? | Toad tells the engine-driver a made-up story about children | The engine-driver knows at once that it is Toad | Toad has washed many shirts before in his life | The clerk gives Toad a free first-class ticket home and a bun
test: Why is it funny that the clerk says Toad must be «pretty well known» there? | He means well known as a cheat, not famous | He means Toad is famous for his motor-cars | He means Toad is the best washerwoman in town | He means he has met Toad at a dinner-party
test: What would have happened if Toad had kept his waistcoat on under the gown? | He could have paid for a ticket and gone home by train | He would have been caught at the castle gate | He would have been far too hot in two coats and fainted on the platform | Nothing; he still had no money anywhere
test: «Driver's sus, he's just being nice to get free washing.» (sus = suspicious) What's fairer? | He's kind; a few shirts is a fair swap for a ride | He's a policeman in disguise | He wants Toad's gold sovereigns | He is planning to kidnap Toad and sell him to a circus
test: Emma from Toronto: «Toad paid his train with a loonie.» (loonie = a one-dollar coin) Put it right. | He had no money at all; he rode on the engine for a promise | He paid with a gold sovereign from the aunt | He paid with his gold watch | He paid with the money the girl lent him
test: Why does Toad think sending money will be «the same thing, or better»? | He thinks money can always stand in for doing what he promised | He knows the driver prefers money | He wants to be fair | The driver asked for money instead of washing
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, Toad paid his bill at the Red Lion before he took the car. Where did that money come from? | A small drawer in his dressing-table at Toad Hall | The gaoler's daughter | The washerwoman | The Rat's coat pocket, while he slept
test: Looking back: in chapter V the Mole said he bought things for Mole End with savings and «going without». How is Toad different? | He has always been rich and careless with money | He saves every penny too | He has never had any money of his own at all | He gives everything to the poor
test: Looking back: which friend said in chapter VI, «He did it awfully well», after Toad's trick? | The Rat | The Mole | The Badger | The Otter
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what thrilled Toad so much that he sat in the dusty road murmuring «Poop-poop!»? | A motor-car that raced past them | A train steaming over a bridge | A swan flying low over the river | A brass band marching to the fair
test: ★★★ Careful: who says «Hullo, mother!», and to whom? | The engine-driver, to Toad | Toad, to the washerwoman | The clerk, to the old gentleman | The girl, to her aunt
test: ★★★ Toad says his children are «playing with matches — and upsetting lamps». Why is that a strange thing for a loving parent to say so cheerfully? | It's dangerous, yet he calls them «the little innocents»: he is making it up | Because matches and lamps are toys for children | Because Toad has no lamps at Toad Hall | Because the children are really ducks and cannot hold matches
exam: Identify the impediment which frustrated the prisoner's purchase of a railway ticket. | The absence of money, pocket and waistcoat alike, these having been left in his cell | The refusal of the railway company to sell tickets to persons in a bonnet | The departure of the train before he reached the booking-office | The recognition of the prisoner by the clerk as a fugitive from justice, and his consequent arrest
exam: State the form of address which, according to the narrative, occasioned the prisoner the greatest annoyance. | «My good woman» | «Your Worship» | «Mr. Toad of Toad Hall» | «Little innocent»
exam: Ascertain the terms upon which the engine-driver consented to convey the prisoner. | That the supposed washerwoman should launder a number of his shirts | That the prisoner should pay double the ordinary fare upon arrival | That the prisoner should shovel coal throughout the journey | That the prisoner should surrender himself to the constabulary at the next station
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the prisoner's intentions in respect of the said shirts? | He intended to remit money for their washing, having no intention of washing them himself | He intended to wash them with great care at Toad Hall | He intended to deliver them to the aunt of the gaoler's daughter for laundering | He intended to return them unwashed, accompanied by a letter of apology and an invitation to dine
